  <p>
    Zippers are one of the most common closures in soft goods—bags, jackets, pouches, and product
    prototypes all depend on choosing the right zipper for the opening, the material, and the load.
    Before you sew one in, you need to know how zipper size is specified.
  </p>

  <h2 id="zipper-numbers">What the zipper number means</h2>
  <p>
    The zipper <strong>number</strong> (often written as <strong>#3</strong>, <strong>#5</strong>,
    <strong>#8</strong>, and so on) is the width of the zipper teeth in millimeters, measured across
    the chain when the zipper is closed.
  </p>
  <p>
    For example, a <strong>#5 zipper</strong> has teeth that are about <strong>5&nbsp;mm across</strong>.
    A #3 is finer (~3&nbsp;mm); a #8 or #10 is heavier and suited to thicker materials and higher stress.
  </p>
  <p>
    That single number is the main size code you will see on zipper tape, product specs, and studio
    supply lists. Match the number to the fabric weight and how hard the closure will be pulled—not
    just to how wide the opening looks.
  </p>

